Hi Alessandro ! 

Alessandro Ipe wrote:
> 
> [...] 
> When I am executing a home-made program (in C, for example) and, during its execution, I am trying
> to recompile it, the compiler is failing to overwrite the old executable with the newly created
> one. I presume there is a mechanism in the OS preventing this to append, like a sort of file
> locking during execution... However, is it possible to permanently disable this option ?

This is a general Unix feature. 
AFAIK, the reason is that the OS will fetch code pages for the running 
process from the program file, so that file must not be changed 
while the process is running. 

If you really want to produce a new executable while the old one 
is still running, you should rename the program file. 
You might also delete it (= still on disk, but not reachable by name). 

HTH, 
Joerg Bruehe 

