Hi ! 

Arthur Dalessandro wrote:
> 
> I've seen the man test pages, which show if a file exists, is there a quick
> and dirty way of checking whether a given directory contains any files?  I
> have a cron job that I want to run, the job should only be run if these temp
> files exist in the given directory.  Any help would be appreciated.

Several ways (in shell): 

   find $DIR -type f -print 
yields results only if there are files (somewhere) _below_ that directory. 
I am no expert in the '-prune' etc. options to restrict it to 
files _in_ that directory, but that should be possible. 

   cd $DIR 
   echo * | cut -c1 
(minus c one) yields "*" if there are no entries, the first character 
of the (alphabetically) first file if there are entries. 
(Fails if you have entries starting with '*' - which were bad design IMO.) 
This does _not_ differ between files and subdirectories! 

Shell script: 
  # echo_first 
  echo $1 
called as 
  echo_first * 
works similar to previous approach, useful in "test" command: 
  cd $DIR 
  if [ `echo_first *` != '*' ] 
  then ... 

Both last approaches fail if the directory contains so many files 
that "*" expands to too many characters, 
and also if you want to detect "hidden" files (name starting with '.'). 

Note that testing "*" directly gives syntax errors if there are two 
or more files, you need to restrict it to a single word. 

Regards 
and happy scripting ! 

Joerg Bruehe 

