Hi Graham,

Before I forget to mention it, make sure you get a return privilege on
such a keyboard.  DEC machines can be very fussy about keyboards.

As for AdvFS, I've been using it for many years.  Tried the GUI once and
never used it again but it looks like it might be fine for someone new
to UNIX; I kill the AdvFS daemon on system startup so it doesn't eat CPU
time.  You won't need the GUI since you've already got a UNIX
background.  See http://tru64unix.compaq.com/unix/advfs.html for a
description of the utilities.  I would recommend them strongly because
they let you do the "interesting stuff."

Make sure you thoroughly understand the way AdvFS works before you plan
large disk configurations.  I've seen some large sites send themselves
right 'round the bend, by blindly adding more and more filesets to the
same domain for example because it was convenient to do so rather than
plan properly.  The doc sets are online: Beginning at
http://www.compaq.com go to Support/Documentation/Tru64/(choose a
version)/System and Network Management/AdvFS Admin.

V5.1 AdvFS no longer uses its own cache (AdvfsCacheMaxPercent in
sysconfig) -- instead it now shares the UBC.  Something to be aware of
in case you're running that version of the OS.  Earlier versions
double-buffer through the AdvFS cache and the UBC.

Hope this helps,
WJD


Sent via Deja.com http://www.deja.com/
Before you buy.


