In article <39F54C21.A8862498@fu-berlin.de>,
	Uwe M�ller <umue@fu-berlin.de> writes:

> I have tried to setup the system power management with dxpower, which
> only allows for spinning down the disks but without any sucsess.
> Editing the /etc/sysconfigtab under header pwrmgr had also no effect
> after rebooting.
> Basically I would like to use DPMS of the monitor and the spin down of
> the disks.

I don't spin down my disks, but I do successfully use DPMS.

First, what version of the operating system are you using, and what
kind of graphics card and monitor do you have?  Those things affect
whether or not DPMS is supported by your configuration.

On my XP1000 system (currently running Tru64 5.1, but this also worked
in V5.0 as well), I can type:

	xset dpms force off

That command will turn on the DPMS feature of my monitor (making the
screen blank and my monitor's power led turn from green to red).  This
is because my Elsa Gloria Synergy has DPMS support that is activated
in the X server in that release of Tru64.  (I think you need to have
the "comet" driver in your system config for this to work; it's
included in V5.x releases.)  Press any key (like the shift key) to
turn the monitor back on.

If this works for you, then you just need to put:

	xset +dpms

into some X startup script so that the X server will use the DPMS
features.

Also, newer versions of xlock (xlockmore?) support DPMS in its
operations.  Find any X "contrib" ftp site (gatekeeper.dec.com,
ftp.x.org) to find this utility.

I don't use console mode much at all, so I cannot tell you how to
activate this outside of the X environment.

