Stefan Schwarzer wrote:
> 
> We use an application (maple) that seems to rely on a (help) database
> which it locks during use. Somehow, such a lock does not have been
> properly removed maybe due to a crash, or a NFS locking problem or
> some other reason. Now, whenever I use the application, it freezes on
> me, probably waiting for the lock to become available.
> 
> Can I find out which process owns a lock?
> Is there any way to unlock the file (if I could find out which one it is)
>  without reboot?
> Over NFS, can I trick the lockd's into loosing locks?


Assuming you have the source and if the lock is a semaphore, you can set
the SEM_UNDO flag when the lock is aquired so that when a process
terminates the lock is guaranteed to be dropped:

        partdb->sops.sem_num = 0;
        partdb->sops.sem_op  = -1;
        partdb->sops.sem_flg = SEM_UNDO;
        if (semop(partdb->sem_id, &partdb->sops, 1) == -1)
                perror("semop (lock)");

If you have no source, and again assuming semaphores, use ipcs(1) to get
the current semaphores and then use ipcrm(1) to remove it.

Tony V.
Kernel Group



