I do think different crons behave differently.  I've misconfigured 
crontab entries under IRIX and Tru64, and my recollection is that IRIX 
cron mails the user complaining that such and such command can't be 
found, while Tru64 cron just ignores it and goes about its business (but 
presumably logs it in /usr/var/adm/cron/log although I've never looked 
in there).  But, it's been awhile since I messed with our crontabs, so I 
could be mistaken.  

> Thanks in advance for any advice..

I'm not sure I completely understand your setup, but this may be 
applicable:

I have one script that scans all our machines for modified files in the 
wee hours of the morning and figures out how much space they will occupy 
if we back them up.  All the machines have that script and the same cron 
files, but obviously each machine doesn't need to do the calculation, so 
the script starts with this:

if ( "${HOST}" != "proline.chem.cornell.edu" ) then
        echo "This script must be run on proline."
        exit
endif

I like this approach because it makes it easier to clone machine 
configurations.  The entry in the cron file redirects script output to 
/dev/null, otherwise I believe cron will mail the output of the job to 
the user, which in this case is not desirable (but the message still 
needs to be there in case the script is run manually).

