With some raid controllers (hsg80, I don't know about swxcr) you can take a
snapshot of disks.  If you can do that, you'd want to halt UNIX, take the
snapshot, and run the install.  If it fails, you've got the snapshot.
If you use vdump and vrestore (properly), you will backup all the files and
attributes.  There's no point to backing up disks, since the majority of data
in a disk is unused garbage anyway; the important thing is to backup your files
and attributes, so vdump and vrestore are good to use.  If you lose the
metadata, who cares?  That is only used in the event that exactly one disk
fails in the set, and you've got an available disk in your spareset.  As soon
as you start a new raid set, it will all be reconstructed again anyway.

As an example of how to use vdump and vrestore, the following command will copy
the /mnt/source filesystem to the /mnt/dest filesystem.
vdump -0f - -D /mnt/source | vrestore -xf - -D /mnt/dest
That's zero-f, not oh-f.
alternatively, It might be more useful to dump into a backup file:
vdump -0f /mnt/backup.bin -D /usr
vrestore -xf /mnt/backup.bin -D /usr
Remember that vdump will not traverse the virtual file system VFS; it will
traverse the file system FS.  So if you do "vdump -0f - -D /" vdump will not go
into /usr, /var, /mnt, /floppy, or anything else that is mounted separately
from root.  You have to use vdump for each mountpoint.

There are two more catches:
One:  It's a good idea to make the dump while UNIX isn't booted.  I think.  So,
boot from your installation CD, and when prompted, cancel the installation in
order to get a prompt.  You will have to manually mount every FS that you want
to backup, so be sure to write that down ahead of time.
Two: Suppose something fails, and you need to restore.  You need to boot
SOMEthing that can run vdump and/or vrestore.  So boot from your installation
CD, and and when prompted ... etc etc.

That's just my two cents.

Remember:  It's not about backing up disks.  Backing up disks is useless.  It's
about backing up your data.

