Hi!

Henry LeRoy Miller, writes:

> I adjusted the max-per-proc & per-proc data-size & address-space
> variables using the dxkerneltuner, setting them to 10 times larger
> than the default.

The values you posted should be large enough. 

One other idea:

How large is your swapspace and what mode of swapping are you using?

The system is capable of running in two different modes: eager and
deferred.  If the link /sbin/swapdefault exists and 'swapon -s' has a
'Reserved' line, you are using eager-mode, which is the default.  You
might want to remove the link and reboot to switch to deferred-mode and
try your application again.

The two modes should be documented in the manuals. In short: 

* eager reserves swapspace when the memory is allocated to be sure it is
  available
* deferred only uses the swapspace when it is really needed.  This might
  result in random processes being killed when you run out of swapspace.


-- 
Stefan



